  "output": "I now have a full picture of the codebase. This is an early-stage Next.js app (Canon) — mostly static marketing/landing pages plus one real data page (`/sources`). The classic \"N+1 query / re-render storm\" problems don't exist yet simply because there isn't enough dynamic surface area yet. Here's what I found that's real and actionable, plus the few genuine risks worth heading off now before the app grows.\n\n## 1. No N+1 query patterns exist today — but there's a duplicate-query trap waiting\n\n`lib/server/sources.ts` currently has:\n\n```ts\nexport async function listSources(): Promise<Source[]> {\n  await connection();\n  const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n  return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n}\n\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources();  // re-queries the DB\n  return { crm: ..., warehouse: ..., synthetic: ... };\n}\n```\n\n`app/(console)/sources/page.tsx` calls `listSources()` directly, and `app/get-started/page.tsx` calls `countSourcesBySide()` (which calls `listSources()` again internally). Right now no single request calls both, so there's no duplicate query *yet* — but the moment someone adds a source count badge to the sources page (a very likely next feature), you'll fire two identical `SELECT * FROM sources` queries in the same request.\n\n**Fix now, cheaply, with React's per-request `cache()`:**\n\n```ts\nimport { cache } from \"react\";\n\nexport const listSources = cache(async (): Promise<Source[]> => {\n  await connection();\n  const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n  return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n});\n```\n\nThis memoizes per-request, so `listSources()` + `countSourcesBySide()` in the same render tree collapse into one query for free, with zero call-site changes.\n\n## 2. `countSourcesBySide` does the count in JS instead of SQL\n\nIt pulls every row and `.filter()`s three times in memory. Fine at current scale (small internal tool, likely dozens of sources), but it's the kind of thing that becomes a real cost if `sources` ever grows or this pattern gets copied to `entities`/`matches` (which *will* be large — that's the whole point of the engine). Worth a SQL aggregate once sources scale:\n\n```ts\nimport { sql } from \"drizzle-orm\";\n\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const rows = await getDb()\n    .select({ kind: sources.kind, count: sql<number>`count(*)::int` })\n    .from(sources)\n    .groupBy(sources.kind);\n  // fold rows into { crm, warehouse, synthetic }\n}\n```\n\nNot urgent — just noting it so the pattern doesn't get copy-pasted onto `entities` later, where a full-table-scan-then-filter-in-JS would actually hurt.\n\n## 3. No unnecessary re-renders — genuinely clean\n\nI checked every client component (`nav-rail.tsx`, `get-started-hero.tsx`, `pipeline-strip.tsx`, `landing-hero.tsx`, `step-list.tsx`). All are either:\n- Static, prop-stable lists rendered once (`PipelineStrip`, `AuditSpecimen`, `DiffRowSpecimen` use `const X = [...] as const` module-level, not recreated per render), or\n- `NavRail`, which re-renders on route change via `usePathname()` — correct and necessary, and the list is 5 items, so this is a non-issue.\n\nNothing here uses `useState`/`useEffect` with a mutable dependency that could cause a re-render loop. There's no `React.memo` needed anywhere given the tiny component trees.\n\n## 4. Caching: only one real opportunity, and it's currently opted out of on purpose\n\n`listSources()` calls `await connection()` to force dynamic rendering — a deliberate, documented choice (see the comment: \"configured sources are request-time state, not build-time\"). That's correct for now. The only thing worth flagging: if `/sources` traffic grows, this means every page load re-hits Postgres with no caching layer at all (no `unstable_cache`, no HTTP cache headers). Given sources change rarely (a human adds a CRM/warehouse connection), a short-TTL cache invalidated on write would be a reasonable future addition — but not needed at current scale, and doing it now would just be premature complexity.\n\n## 5. Memory leaks: none found, and the one place that matters is already handled correctly\n\n`lib/db/index.ts` caches the Postgres connection pool on `globalThis` specifically to survive Next.js HMR reloads in dev — this is the standard, correct fix for the classic \"dev server opens a new connection pool on every file save\" leak. No `useEffect` subscriptions, timers, or listeners anywhere lack cleanup (there are none yet, since no component has one).\n\n## 6. Redundant computations: nothing to flag\n\n`SourceTable`'s `source.config.objects.join(\", \")` runs per row per render — trivial cost, not worth memoizing at this row count. No expensive derived state, no unmemoized filters on large arrays in render paths.\n\n---\n\n**Bottom line:** the codebase is too early-stage to exhibit the failure modes you're asking about (there's exactly one table being queried, and one client-rendered list). The one concrete fix worth making now is wrapping `listSources` in React's `cache()` (item 1) — cheap, prevents a near-certain future duplicate query, and matches the existing code style. Everything else is a \"watch for this as `runs`/`entities`/`conflicts` pages get built\" note rather than a bug today — those tables are the ones that will actually need pagination, indexed lookups, and real caching once the engine starts writing to them.\n",
